 ## Background
-RenderNet was introduced by [S2, unofficial] as a deep convolutional network for differentiable rendering from 3D shapes. It was designed to address the challenges of rendering complex 3D shapes by learning visual feature mappings from exemplary images [S1, unofficial]. The architecture has been used in applications such as scene relighting, novel view synthesis, facial and body reenactment, and photorealistic avatars [S1, unofficial]. It has also been extended to controllable neural voxel rendering for handling high-frequency and complex textures [S1, unofficial].
+RenderNet was introduced as a deep convolutional network for differentiable rendering from 3D shapes [S2, unofficial]. The architecture enables differentiable rendering of voxelized 3D shapes by combining a 3D and a 2D convolutional part connected by a projection unit [S1, unofficial]. It has been applied to scene relighting, novel view synthesis, facial and body reenactment, and photorealistic avatars [S1, unofficial]. The projection unit has also been extended to controllable neural voxel rendering for handling high-frequency and complex textures [S1, unofficial].
 
